1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:03,280
What's the date today?

2
00:00:03,717 --> 00:00:07,797
Easy question. Unless you live in Paris in the spring of 1794.

3
00:00:08,250 --> 00:00:11,290
Because for you, today is not the eighth of May.

4
00:00:11,750 --> 00:00:14,710
Today is the nineteenth of Floréal, Year Two.

5
00:00:15,150 --> 00:00:19,470
There are no Sundays anymore. There's no "Monsieur" or "Madame."

6
00:00:19,917 --> 00:00:23,597
And on some official clocks, noon is "five o'clock."

7
00:00:24,050 --> 00:00:29,010
This is Paris during the Revolution, at the height of what history calls the Terror.

8
00:00:29,450 --> 00:00:33,770
Most videos about it talk about kings, speeches, and the guillotine.

9
00:00:34,217 --> 00:00:38,137
Today, we'll live one ordinary day, as an ordinary Parisian.

10
00:00:38,583 --> 00:00:43,543
Waking up. Buying bread. Going to work. Going to a meeting at night.

11
00:00:43,983 --> 00:00:49,743
And, at the end of the day, watching one of the greatest scientists in history ride past, on his way to die.

12
00:00:50,183 --> 00:00:52,663
But first, let me explain that date.

13
00:00:54,467 --> 00:00:59,267
In 1792, France abolished its monarchy and became a republic.

14
00:00:59,717 --> 00:01:03,077
The revolutionaries believed they were starting history over.

15
00:01:03,517 --> 00:01:06,477
So they decided time itself should start over too.

16
00:01:06,917 --> 00:01:10,117
In late 1793, they adopted a new calendar.

17
00:01:10,583 --> 00:01:17,543
Year One began on the twenty-second of September, 1792, the day after the Republic was proclaimed.

18
00:01:17,983 --> 00:01:20,463
Every month had exactly thirty days.

19
00:01:20,917 --> 00:01:25,077
And the months got new names, inspired by nature and the seasons.

20
00:01:25,517 --> 00:01:33,677
Vendémiaire, the month of the grape harvest. Brumaire, the month of fog. Nivôse, the month of snow.

21
00:01:34,117 --> 00:01:37,077
Floréal, our month, is the month of flowers.

22
00:01:37,517 --> 00:01:39,597
And the seven-day week was gone.

23
00:01:40,050 --> 00:01:43,890
Instead, there were ten-day weeks, called décades.

24
00:01:44,350 --> 00:01:51,870
The days were simply called "first day," "second day," and so on, in Latin-style names, up to décadi, the tenth day.

25
00:01:52,317 --> 00:01:54,237
Décadi was the day of rest.

26
00:01:54,683 --> 00:01:55,643
Do the math.

27
00:01:56,083 --> 00:02:00,563
Before, workers rested one day in seven, plus many saints' days.

28
00:02:01,017 --> 00:02:03,257
Now, they rested one day in ten.

29
00:02:03,717 --> 00:02:05,157
Not everyone was thrilled.

30
00:02:05,617 --> 00:02:11,617
Each day was named after a plant. Every fifth day after an animal, and every tenth after a tool.

31
00:02:12,083 --> 00:02:20,883
Today, the nineteenth of Floréal, is the day of the orache, a garden plant. Tomorrow, the tenth day, is the day of the hoe.

32
00:02:21,350 --> 00:02:24,390
The revolutionaries even tried to change the clock.

33
00:02:24,850 --> 00:02:32,050
In their decimal time, a day had ten hours, each hour a hundred minutes, each minute a hundred seconds.

34
00:02:32,517 --> 00:02:36,597
Midnight was ten o'clock. Noon was five o'clock.

35
00:02:37,050 --> 00:02:43,130
A few clockmakers actually built decimal clocks. Some had both dials, old and new, on the same face.

36
00:02:43,583 --> 00:02:48,223
It never caught on. Decimal time was abandoned in 1795.

37
00:02:48,683 --> 00:02:51,163
But one part of this project did survive.

38
00:02:51,617 --> 00:02:57,697
The same people were busy inventing a new way to measure the world: the meter, the gram, the liter.

39
00:02:58,150 --> 00:03:01,590
Remember that. It will matter at the end of our day.

40
00:03:03,400 --> 00:03:10,360
Our Parisian wakes up before dawn, in a small room in the Faubourg Saint-Antoine, in the east of the city.

41
00:03:10,817 --> 00:03:14,257
This neighborhood is famous for its furniture makers, and for its rebels.

42
00:03:14,717 --> 00:03:19,837
Its people helped storm the Bastille, just around the corner, five years earlier.

43
00:03:20,283 --> 00:03:25,483
Our man is a carpenter. He's in his thirties, with a wife and two kids, all in one room.

44
00:03:25,950 --> 00:03:29,150
Paris has around six hundred thousand people.

45
00:03:29,617 --> 00:03:36,097
Most of them live like this: crowded, in narrow streets, in old buildings several floors high.

46
00:03:36,550 --> 00:03:38,070
There's no running water.

47
00:03:38,517 --> 00:03:44,437
Water comes from public fountains, or from water carriers who climb the stairs with buckets, for a fee.

48
00:03:44,883 --> 00:03:49,763
Much of it comes straight from the Seine. The same river that takes the city's waste.

49
00:03:50,217 --> 00:03:53,817
On the front door of the building, there's a sheet of paper.

50
00:03:54,283 --> 00:03:57,243
It lists the name of every person living inside.

51
00:03:57,683 --> 00:04:03,043
That's the law now. The authorities want to know who lives where, and who is new in town.

52
00:04:03,483 --> 00:04:09,803
In a few weeks, our carpenter will check that list carefully. Just to make sure nothing about it looks suspicious.

53
00:04:11,600 --> 00:04:14,240
Getting dressed in 1794 is a political act.

54
00:04:14,683 --> 00:04:19,483
Our carpenter puts on long trousers, not the knee breeches of the rich.

55
00:04:19,950 --> 00:04:24,110
That's why the radicals of Paris were called sans-culottes: "without breeches."

56
00:04:24,550 --> 00:04:28,070
A short jacket. Wooden clogs, or worn shoes.

57
00:04:28,517 --> 00:04:33,317
And on his hat, a cockade: a small rosette in blue, white and red.

58
00:04:33,783 --> 00:04:36,183
For him, it's a proud symbol.

59
00:04:36,650 --> 00:04:44,170
For his wife, it's also the law. Since September 1793, women in public must wear the tricolor cockade.

60
00:04:44,617 --> 00:04:50,457
On special days, he might wear a red woolen cap, the "bonnet rouge," the cap of liberty.

61
00:04:50,917 --> 00:04:56,837
Silk, powdered wigs, fancy buckles? Those are dangerous now.

62
00:04:57,283 --> 00:04:59,203
They make you look like an aristocrat.

63
00:04:59,650 --> 00:05:04,930
And looking like an aristocrat is a very bad idea in Paris, in 1794.

64
00:05:06,733 --> 00:05:10,173
Before going to work, someone in the family has to get the bread.

65
00:05:10,617 --> 00:05:13,257
And bread is the center of life in Paris.

66
00:05:13,717 --> 00:05:17,717
For a working family, bread could take up a huge share of the budget.

67
00:05:18,183 --> 00:05:22,903
So when bread is scarce or expensive, Paris gets angry.

68
00:05:23,350 --> 00:05:27,990
Many historians see bread prices as one of the sparks of the Revolution itself.

69
00:05:28,450 --> 00:05:33,730
By 1794, the government has set maximum prices for bread and many other goods.

70
00:05:34,183 --> 00:05:36,023
It's called the Maximum.

71
00:05:36,483 --> 00:05:38,563
In theory, this protects the poor.

72
00:05:39,017 --> 00:05:44,697
In practice, many merchants hide their goods, or sell them in secret, at higher prices.

73
00:05:45,150 --> 00:05:47,950
Hoarding food has been made punishable by death.

74
00:05:48,417 --> 00:05:53,057
And Paris has a system of bread cards, to share the bread among families.

75
00:05:53,517 --> 00:05:57,437
So, before dawn, the lines form outside the bakeries.

76
00:05:57,883 --> 00:06:02,043
Mostly women. Waiting, talking, complaining, gossiping.

77
00:06:02,483 --> 00:06:04,883
These lines are where Paris gets its news.

78
00:06:05,350 --> 00:06:10,230
Who was arrested yesterday. Which baker is cheating. What happened at the Convention.

79
00:06:10,683 --> 00:06:12,843
Even the bread itself is political.

80
00:06:13,283 --> 00:06:23,203
In November 1793, the Convention decreed that bakers could make only one kind of bread for everyone, rich and poor: the "bread of equality."

81
00:06:23,650 --> 00:06:27,490
No more white bread for the wealthy and dark bread for the rest.

82
00:06:27,950 --> 00:06:29,950
And what about coffee and sugar?

83
00:06:30,417 --> 00:06:40,977
Since the slave revolt in the French colony of Saint-Domingue, today's Haiti, began in 1791, sugar and coffee have become scarce and expensive.

84
00:06:41,417 --> 00:06:45,737
Parisians rioted over sugar prices in 1792.

85
00:06:46,183 --> 00:06:52,183
So breakfast is bread, maybe soaked in a thin soup. And, if you're lucky, a little wine.

86
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:56,320
Our carpenter walks to his workshop.

87
00:06:56,783 --> 00:06:59,343
On the way, he says hello to his neighbor.

88
00:06:59,783 --> 00:07:03,303
Not "Bonjour, Monsieur." That's suspicious now.

89
00:07:03,750 --> 00:07:06,790
It's "Salut, citoyen." Hello, citizen.

90
00:07:07,250 --> 00:07:11,490
And he tries to use "tu," the informal "you," even with strangers.

91
00:07:11,950 --> 00:07:18,350
In October 1793, a deputy asked the Convention to make "tu" the law.

92
00:07:18,817 --> 00:07:24,577
The Convention refused, because a law like that would make anyone who slipped look suspicious.

93
00:07:25,017 --> 00:07:28,297
But many clubs and local groups adopted it on their own.

94
00:07:28,750 --> 00:07:33,630
He passes walls covered in posters: new laws, decrees, lists of names.

95
00:07:34,083 --> 00:07:38,243
Newspapers are read aloud on street corners, for those who can't read.

96
00:07:38,683 --> 00:07:40,603
The street names are changing too.

97
00:07:41,050 --> 00:07:45,130
Anything that sounds like a king or a saint is being renamed.

98
00:07:45,583 --> 00:07:50,863
The famous Palais-Royal is now the Palais-Égalité: the Palace of Equality.

99
00:07:51,317 --> 00:07:53,317
Even churches have changed.

100
00:07:53,783 --> 00:07:57,623
In late 1793, the churches of Paris were closed for worship.

101
00:07:58,083 --> 00:08:00,803
Notre-Dame was turned into a Temple of Reason.

102
00:08:01,250 --> 00:08:05,730
Many church bells had already been taken down and melted to make cannons.

103
00:08:06,183 --> 00:08:09,543
So the city sounds different too. Far fewer bells.

104
00:08:11,333 --> 00:08:15,653
The workshop is small: a master, a few journeymen, an apprentice.

105
00:08:16,117 --> 00:08:18,517
They make chairs, tables and cabinets.

106
00:08:18,983 --> 00:08:22,263
But fewer rich clients buy fine furniture these days.

107
00:08:22,717 --> 00:08:26,157
Many of them have fled the country. Others are in prison.

108
00:08:26,617 --> 00:08:29,177
So workshops take whatever work they can get.

109
00:08:29,617 --> 00:08:32,977
And there's one huge customer: the war.

110
00:08:33,417 --> 00:08:36,137
France is at war with most of Europe.

111
00:08:36,583 --> 00:08:40,023
Hundreds of thousands of young men have been drafted into the army.

112
00:08:40,483 --> 00:08:44,483
Paris workshops make guns, uniforms, shoes and wagons for them.

113
00:08:44,950 --> 00:08:46,790
Even the soil is drafted.

114
00:08:47,250 --> 00:08:53,730
Citizens are asked to scrape the walls of cellars and stables, to collect saltpeter, an ingredient for gunpowder.

115
00:08:54,183 --> 00:08:57,623
The pay is in paper money, called assignats.

116
00:08:58,083 --> 00:09:01,043
And every month, the assignat buys less.

117
00:09:01,483 --> 00:09:03,483
Our carpenter knows this well.

118
00:09:03,950 --> 00:09:07,710
His wage seems fine on paper. At the bakery, it's a different story.

119
00:09:09,500 --> 00:09:12,220
At midday, the work stops for a meal.

120
00:09:12,683 --> 00:09:18,283
Bread, of course. Maybe some vegetables, cheese, or a soup.

121
00:09:18,750 --> 00:09:22,110
Meat is rare and expensive for a family like this.

122
00:09:22,550 --> 00:09:26,070
At the table, the workers talk. But carefully.

123
00:09:26,517 --> 00:09:30,597
Because in 1794, a careless word can be dangerous.

124
00:09:31,050 --> 00:09:35,850
In September 1793, the Convention passed the Law of Suspects.

125
00:09:36,317 --> 00:09:43,597
It allowed the arrest of anyone who, by their conduct, their words or their writings, showed themselves to be an enemy of liberty.

126
00:09:44,050 --> 00:09:45,890
That is a very wide net.

127
00:09:46,350 --> 00:09:53,310
To keep a job, to get a passport, or just to prove you're a good citizen, you might need a certificate of civism.

128
00:09:53,750 --> 00:09:58,550
A piece of paper, issued by your local section, saying that you are a loyal patriot.

129
00:09:59,017 --> 00:10:02,137
No certificate? You become a suspect.

130
00:10:02,583 --> 00:10:04,583
So people watch what they say.

131
00:10:05,050 --> 00:10:07,050
Neighbors watch neighbors.

132
00:10:07,517 --> 00:10:13,757
Old disputes with a landlord, a rival or an ex-partner can suddenly become denunciations.

133
00:10:15,567 --> 00:10:18,927
While our carpenter works, his wife works too.

134
00:10:19,383 --> 00:10:25,943
Maybe she sells vegetables at a market, sews for a shop, or washes clothes in the Seine.

135
00:10:26,383 --> 00:10:28,863
The Revolution has changed some things for her.

136
00:10:29,317 --> 00:10:35,477
Since 1792, French couples can get divorced. And many women in Paris have used that right.

137
00:10:35,917 --> 00:10:41,197
Women march, sign petitions, and fill the public galleries of the political clubs.

138
00:10:41,650 --> 00:10:43,010
But they can't vote.

139
00:10:43,450 --> 00:10:48,170
In October 1793, the Convention banned women's political clubs.

140
00:10:48,617 --> 00:10:56,137
A few days later, the writer Olympe de Gouges, who had published a Declaration of the Rights of Woman, was guillotined.

141
00:10:56,583 --> 00:10:59,383
The Revolution talked a lot about equality.

142
00:10:59,850 --> 00:11:01,610
It didn't extend it to everyone.

143
00:11:03,400 --> 00:11:07,320
In the late afternoon, there's a sound in the Rue Saint-Honoré.

144
00:11:07,783 --> 00:11:10,183
Wooden wheels on stone.

145
00:11:10,650 --> 00:11:16,250
Carts are coming from the Conciergerie, the prison next to the court of the Revolutionary Tribunal.

146
00:11:16,717 --> 00:11:20,397
They're carrying people who were sentenced to death earlier today.

147
00:11:20,850 --> 00:11:25,650
They roll along the Rue Saint-Honoré, toward the Place de la Révolution.

148
00:11:26,117 --> 00:11:28,757
Today, we call it the Place de la Concorde.

149
00:11:29,217 --> 00:11:35,217
On the way, they pass very close to a house where a lawyer named Maximilien Robespierre rents a room.

150
00:11:35,683 --> 00:11:38,883
He's one of the most powerful men in France right now.

151
00:11:39,350 --> 00:11:42,550
In less than three months, he'll make the same trip.

152
00:11:43,017 --> 00:11:45,497
And today, there are many carts.

153
00:11:45,950 --> 00:11:48,750
Twenty-eight tax farmers will die today.

154
00:11:49,217 --> 00:11:54,017
They belonged to a company that collected taxes for the old king, called the General Farm.

155
00:11:54,483 --> 00:11:59,363
Ordinary Parisians hated the tax farmers. Many people won't feel sorry for them.

156
00:11:59,817 --> 00:12:02,937
But one of the men in the carts is not ordinary at all.

157
00:12:04,733 --> 00:12:07,053
His name is Antoine Lavoisier.

158
00:12:07,517 --> 00:12:09,197
He is fifty years old.

159
00:12:09,650 --> 00:12:13,010
And he is one of the greatest scientists who ever lived.

160
00:12:13,450 --> 00:12:19,770
Lavoisier showed that, in a chemical reaction, matter is not created or destroyed. It only changes form.

161
00:12:20,217 --> 00:12:22,377
He named oxygen and hydrogen.

162
00:12:22,817 --> 00:12:27,057
He helped explain what fire really is, and what happens when we breathe.

163
00:12:27,517 --> 00:12:31,117
Many people call him the father of modern chemistry.

164
00:12:31,583 --> 00:12:36,303
And he worked on a commission that was creating the metric system. The meter, the kilogram.

165
00:12:36,750 --> 00:12:39,790
The same system that most of the world uses today.

166
00:12:40,250 --> 00:12:46,570
Until December 1793, when the government purged him from that commission, for political reasons.

167
00:12:47,017 --> 00:12:49,657
But Lavoisier made one big mistake.

168
00:12:50,117 --> 00:12:53,877
To fund his science, he had invested in the General Farm.

169
00:12:54,317 --> 00:12:57,757
That made him a tax collector, in the eyes of the Revolution.

170
00:12:58,217 --> 00:13:01,577
Next to him in the carts is his father-in-law, Jacques Paulze.

171
00:13:02,017 --> 00:13:03,937
They will die on the same day.

172
00:13:04,383 --> 00:13:08,543
His wife, Marie-Anne Paulze Lavoisier, was his partner in the laboratory.

173
00:13:08,983 --> 00:13:13,703
She drew his instruments, translated scientific works, and kept his notes.

174
00:13:14,150 --> 00:13:17,110
Today, she loses her husband and her father.

175
00:13:17,550 --> 00:13:20,030
There's a famous story about the trial.

176
00:13:20,483 --> 00:13:27,603
When someone asked the court to spare a great scientist, the judge supposedly answered: "The Republic has no need of scientists."

177
00:13:28,050 --> 00:13:32,530
It's a great line. But it's an anecdote, and it may never have been said.

178
00:13:32,983 --> 00:13:38,263
The real quote everyone remembers came later, from the mathematician Joseph-Louis Lagrange.

179
00:13:38,717 --> 00:13:44,317
"It took them only an instant to cut off that head, and a hundred years may not be enough to produce another like it."

180
00:13:44,783 --> 00:13:47,743
The guillotine stands in the middle of the square.

181
00:13:48,183 --> 00:13:54,263
There's a crowd. Some people come every day. Others just walk past without looking.

182
00:13:54,717 --> 00:13:57,597
That's perhaps the most disturbing part of the Terror.

183
00:13:58,050 --> 00:13:59,890
It became part of the routine.

184
00:14:00,350 --> 00:14:09,470
Between the spring of 1793 and the summer of 1794, the Revolutionary Tribunal in Paris sentenced more than two thousand six hundred people to death.

185
00:14:09,917 --> 00:14:14,717
Across France, the official death sentences reached about seventeen thousand.

186
00:14:15,183 --> 00:14:18,623
And many more died in prisons, or without any trial at all.

187
00:14:20,433 --> 00:14:22,993
After dinner, our carpenter doesn't go to bed.

188
00:14:23,450 --> 00:14:24,730
He goes to a meeting.

189
00:14:25,183 --> 00:14:29,103
Paris is divided into forty-eight neighborhoods, called sections.

190
00:14:29,550 --> 00:14:34,430
Each one has its own assembly, where citizens debate, vote, and denounce.

191
00:14:34,883 --> 00:14:37,523
The meetings happen in old churches and convents.

192
00:14:37,983 --> 00:14:43,663
To make sure poor workers could attend, the Convention paid poorer citizens forty sous to take part.

193
00:14:44,117 --> 00:14:47,157
Tonight, the topics are the usual ones.

194
00:14:47,617 --> 00:14:54,417
The price of bread. The war. Someone's certificate of civism. A neighbor who said the wrong thing.

195
00:14:54,883 --> 00:14:58,483
The meeting is loud, smoky, and long.

196
00:14:58,950 --> 00:15:02,230
And it's also, in a strange way, exciting.

197
00:15:02,683 --> 00:15:09,723
A carpenter is debating the future of his country, in a room where, a few years ago, only priests and nobles had a voice.

198
00:15:10,183 --> 00:15:12,903
That's the contradiction of 1794.

199
00:15:13,350 --> 00:15:17,190
Ordinary people had more political power than ever before.

200
00:15:17,650 --> 00:15:20,290
And they were more afraid than ever before.

201
00:15:22,100 --> 00:15:28,100
Late at night, our carpenter walks home, under the dim oil lanterns that hang over the streets.

202
00:15:28,550 --> 00:15:32,870
He climbs the stairs. Checks on the kids. Goes to sleep.

203
00:15:33,317 --> 00:15:38,037
Tomorrow is the twentieth of Floréal: décadi, the day of rest.

204
00:15:38,483 --> 00:15:42,323
He doesn't know it yet, but the Terror is almost over.

205
00:15:42,783 --> 00:15:54,223
In June, it gets even worse. In about seven weeks, between the Law of 22 Prairial and Robespierre's fall, the Paris tribunal sentences one thousand three hundred and seventy-six people to death.

206
00:15:54,683 --> 00:15:58,843
In the thirteen months before that, it had sentenced about twelve hundred.

207
00:15:59,283 --> 00:16:05,683
And then, on the ninth of Thermidor, Year Two, the twenty-seventh of July 1794, the Convention turns on Robespierre.

208
00:16:06,150 --> 00:16:11,110
The next day, he rides in a cart down the Rue Saint-Honoré, to the guillotine.

209
00:16:11,550 --> 00:16:15,150
Within a few years, most of the revolutionary experiments disappear.

210
00:16:15,617 --> 00:16:22,737
Decimal time is gone. The calendar lasts until Napoleon ends it, at the start of 1806.

211
00:16:23,183 --> 00:16:27,343
The seven-day week comes back. And so do Monsieur and Madame.

212
00:16:27,783 --> 00:16:29,383
But one thing survives.

213
00:16:29,850 --> 00:16:33,930
The meter. The kilogram. The liter.

214
00:16:34,383 --> 00:16:38,783
The system Lavoisier helped create is now used by almost every country on Earth.

215
00:16:39,250 --> 00:16:45,810
Every time you buy a kilo of rice, or run a hundred meters, you're using a little piece of the French Revolution.

216
00:16:46,250 --> 00:16:49,130
Built, in part, by a man the Revolution killed.

217
00:16:49,583 --> 00:16:55,743
If you enjoyed this day in Paris, tell me in the comments: which day in history should we live next?
